The snippet shows two ways to append an element to a signal that holds an array in an Angular/TypeScript context. The first uses items.mutate(...) and performs an in-place array push, directly modifying the underlying array. The second uses items.update(...) and returns a new array constructed with the spread operator, producing an immutable-style update by creating a fresh array that contains the previous items plus the new element. Both calls accept an arrow-function callback to compute the new state. Key concepts and APIs: Angular Signals (Signal methods mutate and update), JavaScript/TypeScript arrow functions, Array.prototype.push (in-place mutation) and the spread operator (...) to build a new array (immutable update pattern).

// Mit Angular 16
items.mutate(itemsArray => itemsArray.push(newItem));

// Ab Angular 17:
items.update(itemsArray => [itemsArray, ...newItem]);

This snippet shows two ways to update a reactive array-like signal in an Angular/TypeScript context. The first uses items.mutate(fn) to apply an in-place change by calling Array.prototype.push inside a provided arrow function, which mutates the existing array. The second uses items.update(fn) and returns a new array built with the spread operator, producing an immutable-style replacement of the signal’s value. Key concepts: reactive signals/state, in-place mutation versus immutable replacement, arrow functions, Array.prototype.push, and the spread operator ([...]). The code references Angular (comments), implying use of Angular’s writable signal API where mutate modifies the existing value and update replaces it with the function’s return value.

// With Angular 16

items.mutate(itemsArray => itemsArray.push(newItem));



// From Angular 17:

items.update(itemsArray => [itemsArray, ...newItem]);

This TypeScript Angular standalone component demonstrates using the Signals API for reactive state. It declares an object signal invoice = signal({ price: 1 }) and a derived computed signal total that returns invoice().price. The template interpolates total() and includes a button bound to getNewInvoice() via (click). Calling invoice.mutate(inv => inv.price = 42) updates the object's price in-place, which automatically updates the computed total and the rendered view through Angular's reactive change propagation. Key concepts and APIs shown: @Component decorator, standalone components, template event binding, reactive signals and computed selectors, signal() and computed() call semantics (signals/computeds are read by calling them as functions), and mutate() for in-place state updates.

@Component({
  selector: 'app-signal-object-demo',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    <p> Total Invoice Sum: {{total()}}</p>
    <button (click)="getNewInvoice()">GetNewInvoice</button>
  `
})
export class SignalObjectDemoComponent {
  protected invoice = signal({price: 1});
  protected total = computed(() => this.invoice().price);

  getNewInvoice(): void {
    this.invoice.mutate(inv => inv.price = 42);
  }
}

This TypeScript/Angular component defines a simple reactive counter UI using Angular’s component decorator and the Signal reactive primitive. The component is standalone, declares a protected signal property counter = signal(0), and exposes an increase() method invoked by a button click via (click) event binding. The template displays the current counter value using interpolation with counter() (calling the signal to read its value). increase() increments the signal by reading counter() and writing the new value with counter.set(...); an alternative update API (counter.update(...)) is shown commented out. Key concepts and APIs: @Component decorator, standalone component, template interpolation, event binding, TypeScript method/property visibility, and the Signals API (signal(), signal getter invocation, set()/update()) enabling automatic UI updates.

@Component({
  selector: 'app-signal-demo',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
	<p> Counter: {{counter()}}</p>
	<button (click)="increase()">Increase</button>
  `
})
export class SignalDemoComponent {
  protected counter = signal(0);

  increase(): void {
	this.counter.set(this.counter() + 1);
	//this.counter.update(counter => counter + 1);
  }
}

This TypeScript/Angular component implements a reactive flight search UI using Angular’s signal primitives. It declares reactive signals for inputs and results: from, to and flights (typed Flight[]). A computed value flightRoute derives a string route by reading signals. An effect in the constructor subscribes to signal changes and invokes search() whenever dependent signals change. The async search method reads the current signal values (this.from(), this.to()), guards against empty inputs, and performs an awaited service call to fetch matching flights, updating the flights signal with results. Key concepts/APIs: @Component decorator, TypeScript typing, Angular signals, computed, effect, and async/await for asynchronous data fetching. The code demonstrates declarative reactive state and automatic side‑effect execution on dependency changes.

import { computed, effect, signal } from '@angular/core';
[...]

@Component([…])
export class FlightSearchComponent implements OnInit {

  from = signal('Hamburg'); // in Germany
  to = signal('Graz'); // in Austria
  flights = signal<Flight[]>([]);

  flightRoute = computed(() => this.from() + ' to ' + this.to());

  constructor() {
    effect(() => {
      this.search();
    });
  }

  async search() {
    if (!this.from() || !this.to()) return;

    const flights = await this.flightService.findPromise(
      this.from(),
      this.to()
    );
    this.flights.set(flights);
  }

}

The snippet declares deferred content blocks using a directive-style annotation: @defer (when <condition>; prefetch <strategy>) { ... }. Each block encloses content that will not be executed or rendered immediately. The first form defers until a visibility predicate (isVisible) becomes true and can prefetch based on a predicate (prefetchCondition). The second defers until visibility and requests immediate prefetching ("on immediate"). Key concepts are deferred/lazy loading, conditional rendering based on visibility, and prefetching strategies for ahead-of-time resource fetching. The syntax is a declarative template or component directive found in UI frameworks and signals performance-oriented behavior (lazy execution and controlled prefetch) rather than imperative control flow APIs.

@defer (when isVisible; prefetch when prefetchCondition) {

//...

@defer (when isVisible; prefetch on immediate) {

//...

This TypeScript snippet (Angular) wires reactive UI state and an asynchronous flight search. Two signals, from and to, are initialized with 'Hamburg' and 'Graz'. toObservable converts those signals into RxJS observables, which are combined via combineLatest into a single stream of search parameter objects. The combined stream is debounced by 300ms to limit requests, then switchMap invokes flightService.find(from, to) to perform the async lookup. tap operators toggle a loading signal around the request lifecycle. Finally, the resulting flights observable is converted back to an Angular signal with toSignal, providing an initial empty array. Key concepts/APIs: Angular signals, rxjs-interop (toObservable/toSignal), RxJS combineLatest/debounceTime/switchMap/tap, and an injected flightService for HTTP/async data.

import { toObservable, toSignal } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';
[...]

from = signal('Hamburg');
to = signal('Graz');

from$ = toObservable(this.from);
to$ = toObservable(this.to);

flights$ = combineLatest({ from: this.from$, to: this.to$ }).pipe(
  debounceTime(300),
  tap(() => this.loading.set(true)),
  switchMap((combi) => this.flightService.find(combi.from, combi.to)),
  tap(() => this.loading.set(false))
);

flights = toSignal(this.flights$, { initialValue: [] });

This TypeScript/Angular snippet demonstrates lifecycle-aware cleanup using Angular's DestroyRef and an RxJS observable. The constructor injects a DestroyRef and registers a callback via destroyRef.onDestroy(() => doSomethingOnDestroy()), which will run when the host is destroyed; the returned unregisterFn can cancel that registration. In ngOnInit an RxJS interval(1000) observable emits every second, is piped through takeUntilDestroyed(this.destroyRef) to automatically complete when the DestroyRef signals destruction, and is subscribed to log each value. Key concepts and APIs: Angular dependency injection and lifecycle (ngOnInit), DestroyRef.onDestroy for manual destroy callbacks, RxJS interval + subscribe for recurring emissions, and takeUntilDestroyed for automatic unsubscription/cleanup tied to component/service destruction.

constructor(private destroyRef: DestroyRef) {
  const unregisterFn = destroyRef.onDestroy(() =>
      doSomethingOnDestroy()
  );
  // unregisterFn(); // Nimmt onDestroy-Logik zurück
}

ngOnInit(): void {
  interval(1000)
   .pipe(takeUntilDestroyed(this.destroyRef))
   .subscribe(val => console.log('value:', val))
}

This JSON snippet configures the Angular compiler to emit partially compiled library artifacts by setting angularCompilerOptions.compilationMode to "partial". Placed in a TypeScript/Angular project configuration (e.g., tsconfig.json), it instructs the Angular build toolchain to produce intermediate ("partial") compilation output instead of fully AOT‑compiled code. Key concepts include JSON-based compiler configuration, the angularCompilerOptions entry point, and the compilationMode flag. This mode is used when packaging Angular libraries so that certain Angular metadata and factory code are left in a form the consumer build can finalize. The snippet therefore affects how the Angular compiler emits distributable artifacts and influences downstream application builds that consume the library.

{
  // ...
  "angularCompilerOptions": {
    "compilationMode": "partial"
  }
}

This JSON fragment shows a change in an Angular workspace configuration that replaces a single-string defaultCollection entry with a schematicCollections array. The original "defaultCollection": "@angular/material" sets a single default schematic collection for CLI schematic/generator commands. The new "schematicCollections": ["@angular/material"] expresses the same collection as an array, using JSON syntax to list one or more schematic collections. Key concepts: Angular CLI workspace configuration (angular.json), schematics/schematic collections, and JSON structure differences (string vs. array). Practically, it configures which schematic package the CLI should consult when running generate or add commands, and the array form allows enumerating multiple collections.

// Bisher
"defaultCollection": "@angular/material"

// Jetzt
"schematicCollections": ["@angular/material"]

This code declares two zero-argument functions in TypeScript (function signatures include a return type annotation ": undefined"). Both functions produce no meaningful value: demo1 contains no return statement so it falls off the end and yields undefined implicitly, while demo2 uses an explicit bare return; which also returns undefined. There are no parameters, body statements, or side effects in either function. Key concepts shown are function declaration syntax, explicit return type annotations, the distinction between implicit and explicit returns, and the use of the JavaScript/TypeScript undefined value. The annotations communicate the intended result to the type checker and to callers: both functions are typed to return undefined.

function demo1(): undefined {

// no returns

}

function demo2(): undefined {

return;

}

This TypeScript/JavaScript snippet defines a decorator factory, DemoMeta(key, value), which returns a decorator function that writes a metadata entry into the decorator-provided Context object (context.metadata[key] = value). The factory is applied both as a class decorator (@DemoMeta('entity','3270')) and a method decorator (@DemoMeta('demo','42')). The runtime stores metadata on the target under the well-known Symbol.metadata property, so metadata can be read back as DemoClass[Symbol.metadata].entity and DemoClass[Symbol.metadata].demo. Key concepts/API used: decorator factory (higher-order function returning a decorator), the decorator Context parameter, symbol-keyed property for metadata storage (Symbol.metadata), and reflection-style metadata retrieval. The code demonstrates attaching and reading simple string metadata on class/method targets.

function DemoMeta(key: string, value: string) {

return (_: any, context: Context) => {

context.metadata[key] = value;

};

}



@DemoMeta('entity', '3270')

class DemoClass {

@DemoMeta('demo', '42')

demoMethod() {}

}



DemoClass[Symbol.metadata].entity; // '3270'

DemoClass[Symbol.metadata].demo; // '42'

This TypeScript interface declares a typed property accessor pair named "style". It is a type-level contract (no implementation) that requires any implementer to expose a setter taking a string and a getter returning a CSSStyleDeclaration. Key concepts: interface for structural typing, property accessors (get/set) to define read/write behavior, and the DOM Web API type CSSStyleDeclaration which represents an element’s style object. Practically, this describes an object whose style can be assigned via a string (likely an inline CSS text) and read back as a programmatic CSSStyleDeclaration for manipulating individual CSS properties. It documents expected shape and types for consumers and implementers without providing runtime logic.

interface MyElementStyling {

set style(newValue: string);

get style(): CSSStyleDeclaration;

}

This is a declarative UI template that implements a deferred (suspense-like) rendering pattern for asynchronous content. It displays <app-main-content/> when the condition isLoaded is true, and provides three named fallback blocks for other states: @loading shows a "Loading..." paragraph, @placeholder renders an <icon>pending</icon> placeholder, and @error emits a failure message. Key concepts include conditional rendering, asynchronous/deferred loading, progressive rendering with named fallback slots, and component composition via custom element tags. The syntax is a frontend templating directive style used in web UI frameworks (JavaScript/TypeScript), where @defer (when isLoaded) controls which block is rendered based on the load state.

@defer (when isLoaded) {

<app-main-content/>

} @loading {

<p>Loading...</p>

} @placeholder {

<icon>pending</icon>

} @error {

Failed to load

}

This snippet is an HTML-like templating fragment that declares deferred rendering and fallback UI for a custom component. The @defer block schedules insertion of the <app-my-content/> component using an explicit timer (on timer(1500ms)), deferring its rendering by 1.5 seconds. The @loading block defines a fallback view ("...Loading...") that appears only if the deferred work exceeds a short threshold (after 100ms) and is guaranteed to remain visible for at least 150ms. The @placeholder block provides an initial placeholder ("Placeholder") with a minimum display duration of 100ms. Key concepts: declarative asynchronous rendering, progressive/fallback UI, timing controls for debounce and minimum show time, and component composition via a custom element. The syntax is a template directive style layered on HTML/web-component markup.

@defer (on timer(1500ms)) {

<app-my-content/>

}

@loading (after 100ms; minimum 150ms) {

<p>...Loading...</p>

}

@placeholder (minimum 100ms) {

Placeholder

}

This snippet is an HTML/template fragment that defers rendering of a custom component until a specified DOM element becomes visible in the viewport. The @defer (on viewport(trigger)) block declares that <app-my-content/> should be instantiated or hydrated only when the element referenced by #trigger scrolls into view. The trailing <div #trigger style="margin-top: 1500px">Content</div> supplies the observation target and places it far down the page to delay activation. Conceptually this implements lazy-loading / on‑demand hydration to reduce initial work and improve performance. The pattern relies on a framework-level directive syntax and template reference variable (#trigger); such behavior is typically realized using the browser’s IntersectionObserver to detect viewport intersection and then mount the component.

@defer (on viewport(trigger)) {

<app-my-content/>

}

<div #trigger style="margin-top: 1500px">Content</div>

The snippet shows a declarative “@defer” directive used to mark code blocks for delayed execution under two scheduling policies: "on immediate" and "on idle". Functionally, it expresses deferred/asynchronous work: the immediate block is scheduled to run as soon as the runtime allows, while the nested idle block is postponed until idle time. Key concepts visible are deferred execution, task scheduling and prioritization, nested scoping of deferred tasks, and coordination with an event loop or scheduler (e.g., immediate vs. idle callbacks). The syntax is annotation/DSL-like (an attribute or macro-style directive) rather than standard built-in syntax of a well-known language; it indicates a framework or preprocessing step that interprets @defer with policy parameters to manage asynchronous execution ordering.

@defer (on immediate) {

//...

@defer (on idle) {

//...

This is a UI template snippet that renders a list or a fallback message. It iterates over a collection named items, emitting a paragraph for each item that shows the iteration index and the item’s name ("Element Nummer {{$index}}: {{item.name}}"). The loop includes a track clause (track item.id) to provide a stable identity/key for each entry, which is used by the renderer for efficient DOM diffing and keyed updates. An @empty block supplies a fallback ("No items") when the collection is empty. Key concepts shown are template control-flow (loop and else/empty branch), mustache-style interpolation ({{ }}), use of an index variable ({{$index}}), and keyed rendering for performance. The snippet uses a mustache-like templating syntax common in UI frameworks.

@for (item of items; track item.id) {

<p>Element Nummer {{$index}}: {{item.name}}</p>

} @empty {

<p>No items</p>

}

This snippet is a server-side template that conditionally emits HTML based on the value of a variable. It uses templating directives — @switch, @case, and @default — to define three branches: one that renders a div when value equals 1, another that renders a p when value equals 2, and a default span if no case matches. Key concepts: control-flow branching in templates, conditional rendering of HTML fragments, and use of directive-based template syntax. The form of the directives identifies it as Blade-style server-side templating (commonly used with PHP/Laravel), where the template engine evaluates the switch expression and includes only the matching block in the rendered output.

@switch (value) {

@case (1) {

<div>case one</div>

}

@case (2) {

<p>case two</p>

}

@default {

<span>Default-case</span>

}

}

This TypeScript component class is marked with an @Component decorator, indicating it is registered as a UI component. It defines an instance property val initialized to 42 and uses the constructor to schedule two post-render callbacks via afterRender and afterNextRender. Each callback is provided as an arrow function that logs the component’s current val to the console. Key concepts include class-based component structure, decorator metadata, constructor initialization, and use of arrow functions to preserve lexical this when accessing instance state. The afterRender and afterNextRender calls represent lifecycle-scheduling APIs that run code after the component’s render cycle and after the next render cycle, respectively, enabling deferred post-render behavior.

@Component({})

export class DemoComponent {

val = 42;

constructor() {

afterRender(() => {

console.log('afterRender', this.val);

});

afterNextRender(() => {

console.log('afterNextRender', this.val);

});

}

}

This snippet is an HTML-like templating example that declares deferred rendering of custom components based on user events. It uses a @defer directive to postpone inserting or activating <app-my-content/> and <app-my-display/> until specific triggers occur (e.g., "on interaction(trigger)" and "on hover(trigger)"). Triggers are tied to DOM elements referenced by a template variable syntax (#trigger). A @placeholder block provides alternative markup (a trigger button) to render before the deferred content is activated. Key concepts illustrated: declarative lazy/deferred rendering, event-driven activation (interaction and hover), template reference variables for binding triggers to elements, and use of custom web components. The code reads as a markup-level API for progressive or on-demand loading of UI components.

@defer (on interaction(trigger)) {

<app-my-content/>

}

<button #trigger>Trigger</button>



@defer (on interaction) {

<app-my-content/>

} @placeholder {

<button>Trigger</button>

}



@defer (on hover(trigger)) {

<app-my-display/>

}

<button #trigger>Trigger</button>

This snippet is a server-side template conditional that renders one of three HTML fragments depending on boolean expressions. Using a Razor/Blade-style directive syntax (@if, @else if, @else) it evaluates the expression named show first; if true, it outputs the first <div>. If show is false and otherCond is true, it outputs the second <div>. If neither condition is true, it falls back to the final <div>. Key concepts include conditional branching in templates, server-side evaluation of boolean expressions, and inline mixing of template directives with raw HTML so only the selected branch is emitted in the rendered output. The syntax is characteristic of server-side templating engines that use @ directives (e.g., Razor or Blade-like templating).

@if (show) {

<div>case content 1</div>

} @else if (otherCond) {

<div>case content 2</div>

} @else {

<div>other content</div>

}

This snippet is JavaScript/TypeScript code that configures a client-side router by calling provideRouter with a routes collection and an additional behavior wrapper. The wrapper is produced by withViewTransitions and accepts an options object containing an onViewTransitionCreated callback. Conceptually it composes routing and view-transition behavior: the router will use the supplied routes and invoke the provided callback whenever a view transition is created. Key concepts: higher-order functions (provideRouter and withViewTransitions accept other functions/objects), configuration via an options object, and an arrow-function callback (transitionInfo => { }) to handle transition lifecycle events. It implies integration with a view transition facility (exposing transitionInfo) to customize or react to transitions at creation time.

provideRouter(

routes,

withViewTransitions({

onViewTransitionCreated: transitionInfo => {

}

})

),

This TypeScript/JavaScript snippet demonstrates using class-element decorators to attach metadata for a class’s fields and methods. The setMetadata decorator receives the target and a Context object (typed via an interface) whose metadata map and element name are used to store the string "Demo-Data". The decorator is applied to a class field (demoAmount) and a method (demoAction), so both element names become keys in a metadata container placed on the class under Symbol.metadata. The code then reads MyClass[Symbol.metadata] and logs the resulting object as JSON, producing { "demoAmount": "Demo-Data", "demoAction": "Demo-Data" }. Key concepts: decorators, contextual metadata, Symbol-based metadata storage, class fields/methods, and JSON serialization.

interface Context {

name: string;

metadata: Record<PropertyKey, unknown>;

}

function setMetadata(_target: any, context: Context) {

context.metadata[context.name] = 'Demo-Data';

}

class MyClass {

@setMetadata

demoAmount = 123;

@setMetadata

demoAction() { }

}



const ourMetadata = MyClass[Symbol.metadata];

console.log(JSON.stringify(ourMetadata));

// { "demoAmount": "Demo-Data", "demoAction": "Demo-Data" }

This TypeScript snippet declares an ambient function onEvent whose signature expects a callback f: () => undefined and returns undefined. The call onEvent(function f() { }) triggers a compiler error: "Argument of type '() => void' is not assignable to parameter of type '() => undefined'." Key concepts: TypeScript function types and return-type compatibility, the difference between void and undefined, and ambient declarations using declare (no runtime implementation). The error arises because the passed anonymous function has an inferred return type of void (no explicit return), which is not considered assignable to a function type that must return undefined. This demonstrates TypeScript’s structural typing and strict return-type checking for callbacks.

declare function onEvent(f: () => undefined): undefined;



onEvent(function f() { })

// Argument of type '() => void' is not assignable to parameter of type '() => undefined'.

This snippet is an Angular template loop that iterates over a collection called items and renders a paragraph for each element. It uses a structural @for directive with an iteration variable item and a tracking expression (track item.id) to preserve DOM nodes based on item identity. Inside the loop it outputs the current zero‑based index ($index) and item.name via interpolation ({{ }}). An @empty block provides a fallback paragraph when items is empty. Key concepts: template structural directives for iteration, interpolation binding, a track/tracking expression to optimize DOM reuse and change detection, and a conditional empty branch. The code is written in an Angular template (frontend UI templating).

@for (item of items; track item.id) {
  <p>Element Nummer {{$index}}: {{item.name}}</p>
} @empty {
  <p>No items</p>
}

This snippet is an Angular template-level construct that defers rendering of a component until an asynchronous condition is met. Using a @defer expression with a "when isLoaded" predicate, it delays insertion of the <app-main-content/> component until isLoaded is truthy. The block includes named fallback sections: @loading renders a short loading message while the primary content is pending, @placeholder provides an inline placeholder icon, and @error displays a failure message if the deferred content fails to resolve. Key concepts are declarative asynchronous rendering, conditional/suspense-style rendering, template-level fallbacks and error boundaries, and use of custom component elements. The syntax resembles a structural directive in Angular templates for managing streamed or lazy content.

@defer (when isLoaded) {
  <app-main-content/>
} @loading {
  <p>Loading...</p>
} @placeholder {
  <icon>pending</icon>
} @error {
  Failed to load
}

This TypeScript snippet defines an Angular component class that exposes three input properties for parent-to-child data binding. The @Component decorator supplies minimal metadata (an empty inline template), while the @Input decorators mark language, demoResolved, and demoData as public bindable properties. Each property is typed as an optional string (the ? indicates the property may be undefined), enabling parent components to pass values using Angular property binding syntax (for example, [language]="..."). The code demonstrates key Angular/TypeScript concepts: component declaration via decorators, class-based components, decorator-driven input bindings, and static type annotations for safer code. No lifecycle hooks or methods are present; the component solely serves as a data receiver.

@Component({ template: '' })
export class DemoComponent {
  @Input() language?: string;
  @Input() demoResolved?: string;
  @Input() demoData?: string;
}

This snippet is a template-level conditional rendering construct for an Angular component: it evaluates boolean expressions in the component context and renders one of three mutually exclusive DOM fragments. The @if/@else if/@else chain checks `show` first, then `otherCond`, and falls back to the final `@else` block if neither condition is true. Key concepts: template control flow, conditional rendering, and scoped expression evaluation (the conditions reference component properties or methods). Functionally it inserts the corresponding <div> into the DOM and omits the others, enabling branch-specific markup without manual DOM manipulation. It replaces traditional structural directives like *ngIf with a more concise, template-level conditional syntax.

@if (show) {
  <div>Inhalt Fall 1</div>
} @else if (otherCond) {
  <div>Inhalt Fall 2</div>
} @else {
  <div>sonstiger Inhalt</div>
}

This code is an Angular component template snippet that conditionally renders different DOM fragments based on a single expression, value. It uses a declarative switch-case control flow with @switch, @case and @default blocks: when value equals 1 it renders a <div> ("Fall Eins"), when value equals 2 it renders a <p> ("Fall Zwei"), and for any other value it renders a <span> ("Default‑Fall"). Key concepts are template control-flow and conditional rendering in component templates, achieved via structural directives (@switch/@case/@default) rather than imperative logic. The snippet belongs to Angular’s template syntax (used alongside TypeScript components) and demonstrates concise branching inside a template to output different markup depending on runtime state.

@switch (value) {
  @case (1) {
	<div>Fall Eins</div>
  }
  @case (2) {
	<p>Fall Zwei</p>
  }
  @default {
	<span>Default-Fall</span>
  }
}

This TypeScript snippet defines an Angular router configuration: a single route for path 'demo' that renders DemoComponent. The route’s canActivate property is built by concatenating two sources of guards — mapToCanActivate([DemoClassGuard]) and demoFuncGuard — producing the final array of guard handlers the router must run before activating the route. It illustrates mixing class-based guards (passed as class references) with function-style guards and combining them into one canActivate list using JavaScript's concat. Key concepts: Angular routing (Routes array), route guards (canActivate), composition of guards, and use of TypeScript/JavaScript array operations. Guards typically return boolean/UrlTree or asynchronous equivalents (Promise/Observable) to allow or block navigation.

const routes: Routes = [
  {
	path: 'demo',
	canActivate: mapToCanActivate([DemoClassGuard])
                    .concat(demoFuncGuard),
	component: DemoComponent
  }
];

This TypeScript snippet configures a single Angular route and registers the router at application bootstrap. It defines a routes array with a "demo" path mapped to DemoComponent, attaches static route-specific metadata via the data property, and supplies a resolve entry that produces a precomputed value for the route. The call provideRouter(routes, withComponentInputBinding()) registers the router providers with Angular’s dependency injection and enables automatic binding of route values to component inputs via withComponentInputBinding. Key concepts and APIs shown: route configuration (path, component), static route data, resolve-driven prefetching of values, DI-based router registration (provideRouter), and component input binding. The code uses TypeScript within the Angular framework.

const routes = [{
  path: 'demo', 
  component: DemoComponent,
  data: {'demoData': 'My static data'},
  resolve: {'demoResolved': () => 'My resolved data'},
}];

// inside AppModule/app-config
provideRouter(routes, withComponentInputBinding())

This TypeScript snippet defines an Angular component class that acquires a dependency via the standalone inject() DI helper rather than a constructor parameter. The readonly demoService property is assigned the result of inject(DemoService, { optional: true, host: true }). That instructs Angular’s dependency injection system to resolve the DemoService token for this component, but to return no value (null/undefined) instead of throwing if the provider is absent (optional: true), and to constrain the search to the component’s host injector boundary (host: true). Key concepts and APIs shown are Angular’s @Component decorator, the inject() runtime DI accessor, provider token usage (DemoService), and resolution flags (optional, host) in a TypeScript class.

@Component({})
class DemoComponent {
  readonly demoService = inject(DemoService, {
    optional: true,
    host: true
  });
}

This snippet shows a programmatic route navigation that includes a query parameter and the resulting runtime values that land on a routed component. Calling router.navigateByUrl('/demo?language=french') navigates to the /demo route with language=french; the target component’s language property is set to "french". The comments show two additional component properties populated from route configuration: demoData holds static route data ("My static data") and demoResolved holds data provided by a route resolver ("My resolved data"). Key concepts: programmatic navigation, query parameters, and route-provided data (static data and resolved asynchronous data). The style and APIs (router.navigateByUrl, component properties, route data/resolvers) indicate TypeScript code in an Angular routing context.

router.navigateByUrl('/demo?language=french')
// component.language === 'french';
// component.demoData === 'My static data';
// component.demoResolved === 'My resolved data'

This snippet uses TypeScript property decorators to declare component input bindings. Each @Input annotation marks a class field as an externally set input and provides optional metadata: a string or an object with keys like alias and required. Examples show three patterns: @Input('fooAlias') maps an external attribute named "fooAlias" into the local property fooVal; @Input({alias: 'barAlias', required: true}) both renames the binding and flags it as required; @Input({required: true}) keeps the property name and marks it required. All properties are initialized to empty strings. Key concepts: TypeScript decorators (property-level metadata), component input/property binding, aliasing of external attribute names, and a required-flag for input validation semantics. The syntax is idiomatic to Angular-style component frameworks.

@Input('fooAlias') fooVal = '';
@Input({alias: 'barAlias', required: true}) barVal = '';
@Input({required: true}) bazVal = '';

This TypeScript/Angular snippet defines two route guards that decide whether navigation should proceed based on an authentication check. The class-based guard (DemoClassGuard) is decorated with @Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' }) so it is DI-registered at the root and exposes a canActivate(route: ActivatedRouteSnapshot, state: RouterStateSnapshot): boolean | UrlTree method that returns the result of DemoAuthService.isLoggedIn(). The second is a functional guard (demoFuncGuard) typed as CanActivateFn that uses the inject() helper to obtain DemoAuthService and returns isLoggedIn(). Key concepts and APIs shown: dependency injection, @Injectable provider registration, route guard contracts (CanActivate/CanActivateFn), route snapshot types (ActivatedRouteSnapshot, RouterStateSnapshot), UrlTree for redirect results, and a service-based isLoggedIn() authorization check. Note: the class example contains a small naming mismatch between the injected property and the reference used.

@Injectable({providedIn: 'root'})
export class DemoClassGuard {
  constructor(private authService: DemoAuthService) {}
  canActivate(route: ActivatedRouteSnapshot,
              state: RouterStateSnapshot): boolean | UrlTree {
	return this.auth.isLoggedIn();
  }
}

export const demoFuncGuard: CanActivateFn =
  (route, state) => {
     const authService = inject(DemoAuthService)
     return authService.isLoggedIn();
  };

[SECTION]:
Signals
Zoneless Change Detection (since Angular 18.0)

[CODE]:
bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [
    provideExperimentalZonelessChangeDetection(),
  ],
});

Angular 18 introduces Zoneless Change Detection, a new experimental feature that replaces the traditional ZoneJS-based change detection mechanism with a more efficient system built on Signals. Signals are a new reactive primitive designed to track values that change over time and notify Angular precisely when updates occur. Unlike the previous approach where Angular monitored all asynchronous events globally, often triggering unnecessary full application checks, Signals enable fine-grained change detection by directly observing changes at the data-binding level. This reduces redundant checks and improves performance. The slide demonstrates how to enable this feature by adding the provider provideExperimentalZonelessChangeDetection() within the bootstrapApplication function for the AppComponent. This approach aligns with Angular’s goal to simplify its API surface and enhance developer experience by offering a more straightforward, reactive model that integrates well with existing strategies like OnPush and AsyncPipe, marking a significant step toward more efficient and maintainable Angular applications.

[SECTION]:
Signals
API

– Writable Signals

– Computed Signals

– Effects

[CODE]:
const count = signal(0);
count.set(3);

const doubleCount = computed(() => count() * 2);

effect(() => console.log(`The count is: ${doubleCount()}`));

The Signals API in Angular 18 introduces a reactive programming model centered around three core concepts: Writable Signals, Computed Signals, and Effects. Writable signals are created using the signal() function with an initial value, similar to a BehaviorSubject, but updated via the set() method instead of next(). Computed signals derive their values reactively from writable signals using the computed() function, producing read-only signals that automatically update when their dependencies change. This approach aligns with Angular's shift from class-based paradigms toward functional programming, simplifying state management and reactivity. While writable computed signals are under discussion, the current model emphasizes clear separation between mutable state and derived state. Effects enable side effects in response to signal changes, though their use is primarily intended for framework-level operations rather than application code. Together, these primitives enable efficient change detection and automatic propagation of state changes, enhancing performance and developer experience in Angular applications.

[SECTION]:
Ultimate goal of the Angular team so components can work without Zone.js

All components inputs, outputs, and models (2-way bindings) can be signals now

[CODE]:
@Component({
  signals: true,
  ...
})
export class UserProfile {
  // Create an optional input without an initial value.
  firstName = input<string>(); // Signal<string|undefined>

  // Create a model, which is a signal that supports two-way binding.
  active = model(false);

  saved = output<number>(); // EventEmitter<number>
}

Signal-based components in Angular represent a significant shift from traditional change detection mechanisms, eliminating the need for Zone.js. By leveraging signals, Angular precisely tracks dependencies and changes, enabling more efficient and predictable updates. All component inputs, outputs, and models supporting two-way bindings can now be implemented as signals, allowing Angular to build a dependency graph that identifies exactly what needs to be updated when a signal changes. This reactive programming approach enhances state management and change detection by ensuring only affected parts of the component tree are refreshed. The slide’s code snippet exemplifies this by showing how to define a component with signals enabled, including optional input signals, two-way binding model signals, and output event emitter signals. This new paradigm improves performance and clarity by making change detection explicit and fine-grained, moving away from Angular’s previous reliance on broad, implicit change detection triggers.

