The slide introduces signal-based components in Angular, emphasizing the Angular team's goal to eliminate reliance on Zone.js by adopting signals for component state management and event handling. Inputs, outputs, and models, which support two-way bindings, are now implemented as signals using new functions like input and model that replace traditional decorators. This shift enables components to reactively synchronize their state with the UI more efficiently. The Angular framework facilitates this transition by providing migration commands that automatically convert existing decorators to signal-based APIs, simplifying adoption. This approach aligns with Angular's vision of moving away from change detection terminology toward a synchronization model, where data changes directly trigger component updates. The code example demonstrates how to define optional input signals, two-way binding models, and output event emitters, illustrating the practical application of signals to create reactive, streamlined components that integrate seamlessly with Angular's evolving reactive programming paradigm.

